window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-77bf45da3b7c7ac0"]={"key":"ny_main-77bf45da3b7c7ac0","group":"ny_main","uid":"77bf45da3b7c7ac0","rank":7,"title":"authored doc birchmoor inglewood editorial package 2026","occupation":"news_analysts_reporters_journalists","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are the investigations editor preparing Birchmoor Housing Trust’s Friday publication review of tenant-insurance complaints handled by Inglewood Agricultural under the enterprise contract. Maude Zuniga will approve release with legal counsel, but most of the executives reading the package do not know the case history. The current draft compares 1,240 complaints closed in October–December 2025, when 31% were upheld, the median final-response time was 39 calendar days and 86 were later reopened, with 1,410 closed in January–March 2026, when 24% were upheld, the median was 27 days and 143 were later reopened. Inglewood changed its policy on 15 January: complaints opened from that date could close automatically after 14 days of customer inactivity following a request for information, subject to supervisor reopening, while older cases stayed on the previous process through 31 March; unresolved legacy cases move to the new process from 1 April. Frida Kilgore authorised a correction on 30 March because the draft had said the 14 days ran from complaint opening and that closed cases could not be reopened. A confidential source also said, “We were told at the Tuesday pilot call to close every quiet file before month-end, whatever stage it was at,” but the source was the only quality reviewer seconded to the January pilot, so the quotation and that detail together could identify them. Kasper Bassett’s review comment asks us to distinguish what must change before release from what should change as prudent editorial protection, without assuming that every sensible precaution is a legal requirement. Produce a publication-ready executive news and editorial package as `inglewood_complaints_editorial_package.pdf`, no more than six pages including any notes, and write it at the top level of the working directory using exactly that filename. Compare the two periods, explain the material movement without overstating what caused it, apply England’s defamation and privacy rules as of 31 March 2026, incorporate and record Frida’s correction, and give Maude one explicit choice between a correction-led and an accountability-led framing. Use only the case facts, figures and quotation provided here; do not invent interviews, corroboration, figures or legal clearance. Make the package sufficient for executives and counsel to understand, review and act on without coming back to the author.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `inglewood_complaints_editorial_package.pdf` — A publication-ready executive news and editorial package comparing the two complaint-handling periods, documenting the authorised correction, addressing source and legal risk, and making one framing recommendation. (Write the file at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename.; The complete PDF, including notes or legal references, must not exceed six pages.; Use only the case facts, figures and quotation stated in the request.; The rendered PDF must be legible and suitable for executive and legal review.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/inglewood_complaints_editorial_package.pdf","bytes":59797,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/e2b8507548caa09501665192ff1652ce4dcbc9b0b0c9a3876cbde252910ed6d6.pdf","sha256":"e2b8507548caa09501665192ff1652ce4dcbc9b0b0c9a3876cbde252910ed6d6","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"BIRCHMOOR HOUSING TRUST | INTERNAL REVIEW                                                                                                                                             PAGE 1 OF 6\n\n\n\n\nFRIDAY PUBLICATION REVIEW\n\n\nInglewood complaints: release decision\nFor Maude Zuniga, legal counsel and contracting stakeholders | record current to 31 March 2026\n\n\n\n\n    DECISION REQUESTED                                                                                                             WHY PUBLICATION NEEDS APPROVAL\n\n\n    Approve a correction-led, qualified release.                                                                                   The review concerns a contracted tenant-insurance provider,\n                                                                                                                                   a material correction, a mixed policy cohort and an\n    Report the reopening concern, but do not present policy causation or improper closure as established.                          uncorroborated confidential account.\n\n\nPERIOD COMPARISON | SUPPLIED MEASURES AND DERIVED MOVEMENTS\n\n\n\n  Measure                                                                  Oct-Dec 2025             Jan-Mar 2026                          Movement\n\n  Complaints closed                                                        1,240                    1,410                                 +170 (13.7%)\n\n  Upheld                                                                   31%                      24%                                   -7.0 pp\n\n  Median final response                                                    39 calendar days         27 calendar days                      -12 calendar days\n\n  Later reopened                                                           86 (6.9%)                143 (10.1%)                           +57 (+3.2 pp)\n\nReopening shares use each period's closed-complaint volume.\n\n\n\n\n    WHAT THE COMPARISON ESTABLISHES                                                                   WHAT IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH\n\n\n    Higher closure volume, a 7.0 percentage-point lower upheld rate and a                             The January-March period mixes legacy and new-policy cases. The figures support\n    12-calendar-day faster median sit beside a higher later-reopening share: 6.9% to                  scrutiny and a question about process effects; they do not prove that the policy caused\n    10.1% (+3.2 pp).                                                                                  any movement or that files were improperly closed.\n\n\n\n\nInvestigations editorial package | confidential source detail withheld from publishable treatment                                                                                                1/6\n\fBIRCHMOOR HOUSING TRUST | INTERNAL REVIEW                                                                                                                                                              PAGE 2 OF 6\n\n\n\n\nRECORD AND CHRONOLOGY\n\n\nPolicy transition and correction record\nThe first quarter is a transition comparison, not a clean all-new-policy cohort.\n\n\nPOLICY TIMELINE\n\n\n\n\n   Before 15 Jan                                                           15 Jan 2026                                             31 Mar 2026                                                     1 Apr 2026\n   Previous process                                             New policy for complaints opened                            Older cases remain on the old                                 Unresolved legacy cases move to\n                                                                         from this date                                       process through this date                                           the new process\n\n\n\n    CORRECT POLICY MECHANICS\n\n\n    For complaints opened from 15 January 2026, automatic closure can follow 14 days of customer inactivity after a request for information. Supervisor reopening is available.\n    Complaints opened before the change stay on the old process through 31 March; unresolved legacy cases move to the new process on 1 April.\n\n\n\n\n    CORRECTION AUDIT RECORD\n\n\n    Authorised             30 March 2026 | Frida Kilgore\n\n    Superseded draft assertion               The 14-day period ran from complaint opening and closed cases could not be reopened.\n\n    Corrected record                         The 14 days run after an information request and customer inactivity; supervisor reopening is available. Correction incorporated throughout this\n                                             package.\n\n\n\n    OPERATIONAL READING                  The observed pattern can be compared across the periods, but mixed populations and no corroborating material mean it is an association for review, not a\n                                         causal finding. A clean new-process end state begins only after the 1 April legacy transfer.\n\n\n\n\nInvestigations editorial package | confidential source detail withheld from publishable treatment                                                                                                               2/6\n\fBIRCHMOOR HOUSING TRUST | INTERNAL REVIEW                                                                                                                                                      PAGE 3 OF 6\n\n\n\n\nPROPOSED PUBLIC-FACING TREATMENT\n\n\nPublishable copy - conditional on release approval\nThis page is public-facing draft treatment only. Internal approval and legal guidance begins on the next page.\n\n\n\n    PUBLIC-FACING DRAFT | FACTS, CALCULATIONS, ATTRIBUTED ALLEGATION AND INTERPRETATION KEPT DISTINCT\n\n\n\n\nHigher reopening share prompts qualified scrutiny during policy transition\nBirchmoor Housing Trust's investigation of complaint handling by its contracted tenant-insurance provider, Inglewood Agricultural, records faster final responses\nand a lower upheld rate in January-March, alongside a higher share of cases later reopened. The period contains cases on different processes, so the figures do\nnot establish why the movements occurred.\n\n\n\nDRAFT BODY\nThe review compares 1,240 closures in October-December 2025 with 1,410 in January-March 2026: +170 (13.7%). The upheld rate moved from 31% to 24%; median final response\nmoved from 39 to 27 calendar days.\n\nLater reopenings rose from 86 (6.9% of closed complaints) to 143 (10.1%), a +3.2 pp increase in share. The pattern supports scrutiny; it does not prove a policy effect.\n\nFrom 15 January 2026, complaints opened from that date could close automatically after 14 days of customer inactivity following a request for information. Supervisor reopening\nremained available. Older cases stayed on the previous process through 31 March; unresolved legacy cases move to the new process on 1 April.\n\nA confidential source alleged pressure to close inactive files before month-end irrespective of their progress. That account is uncorroborated and is not treated as proof of an instruction,\nimproper closure or the cause of the measured outcomes.\n\n\n\n    COPY DISCIPLINE                      Established: policy mechanics and supplied figures. Calculated: the movements and reopening shares. Allegation: the singular confidential account. Editorial\n                                         interpretation: qualified scrutiny during a transition, not a finding of wrongdoing or causation.\n\n\n\n\nInvestigations editorial package | confidential source detail withheld from publishable treatment                                                                                                       3/6\n\fBIRCHMOOR HOUSING TRUST | INTERNAL REVIEW                                                                                                                                                                   PAGE 4 OF 6\n\n\n\n\nAPPROVAL GUIDANCE\n\n\nRelease blockers and editorial safeguards\nKasper Bassett's requested boundary: mandatory controls are not the same as prudent editorial protection.\n\n\n\n\n    MUST RESOLVE BEFORE RELEASE                                                                                              SHOULD DO AS EDITORIAL PROTECTION\n\n    Release-blocking accuracy, attribution and identification controls                                                       Risk-reducing choices, not automatic legal commands\n\n\n       Use the corrected mechanics everywhere: 14 days follow an information request                                            Lead with the correction and the mixed-cohort limitation, even though the\n       and customer inactivity; supervisor reopening is available; legacy treatment                                             reopening increase remains reportable and important.\n       remains clear.\n                                                                                                                                Show reopening shares with raw counts so readers do not mistake different\n       Keep headline, summary and body within established facts and calculations.                                               complaint volumes for a like-for-like count comparison.\n       Attribute the confidential account as uncorroborated; do not assert improper\n       closure or policy causation as fact.                                                                                     Consider a focused pre-publication opportunity for Inglewood to address the\n                                                                                                                                corrected policy mechanics and the general allegation. No response is assumed or\n       Withhold verbatim wording and granular provenance that could reasonably                                                  reported here.\n       identify the confidential source. The compact paraphrase is the only publishable\n       source treatment in this package.                                                                                        After 1 April, commission a separate assessment of the all-new-process period\n                                                                                                                                rather than treating the transition figures as an endpoint.\n       Have Maude Zuniga and legal counsel approve the final headline, summary and\n       body together. This package assumes no legal clearance has already been given.\n\n\n\n\nBoundary applied: a mandatory item prevents unsupported or unlawfully identifying publication; a should item reduces editorial or reputational risk beyond that minimum.\n\n\nInvestigations editorial package | confidential source detail withheld from publishable treatment                                                                                                                  4/6\n\fBIRCHMOOR HOUSING TRUST | INTERNAL REVIEW                                                                                                                                                                   PAGE 5 OF 6\n\n\n\n\nLEGAL RISK APPLICATION\n\n\nEngland media law: focused publication analysis\nEngland | as of 31 March 2026 | editorial risk analysis only; no formal legal clearance is assumed.\n\n\n\n\n    DEFAMATION                                                                                                            PRIVACY AND CONFIDENTIALITY\n\n    Defamation Act 2013: threshold and defences                                                                           Misuse of private information: two-stage application\n\n    SERIOUS HARM THRESHOLD                                                                                                STAGE 1: REASONABLE EXPECTATION\n\n    Section 1 requires publication to have caused, or be likely to cause, serious harm to                                 Ask whether the source has a reasonable expectation of privacy in identity and\n    reputation. If Inglewood is a body that trades for profit, serious harm means serious                                 contextual details. A workplace account can still attract protection where granular\n    financial loss. Its legal status is not established in this record, so that condition is                              provenance could identify the person.\n    applied only if relevant.                                                                                             STAGE 2: BALANCE\n    SUBSTANTIAL TRUTH\n                                                                                                                          If that expectation exists, balance the source's privacy interest against freedom of\n    The truth defence turns on proving the substantial truth of the imputation conveyed.                                  expression and the public interest in reporting complaint handling. Use only what is\n    The corrected policy and figures can be supported as facts; the source account alone                                  proportionate to communicate the allegation; source protection carries substantial\n    does not prove an instruction, improper closure or causation.                                                         weight in this record.\n    HONEST OPINION                                                                                                        APPLICATION\n\n    Use only for a recognisable opinion with its basis indicated and one an honest person                                 The non-identifying paraphrase preserves the allegation while avoiding details that\n    could hold on the stated facts. It is not a label that converts an unverified allegation into                         could triangulate the source. Publishing attribution as fact, or adding identifying\n    fact.                                                                                                                 context, would materially increase risk.\n    MATTER OF PUBLIC INTEREST                                                                                             KEEP CONCEPTS SEPARATE\n\n    Complaint handling by a contracted tenant-insurance provider can be a public-interest                                 A confidentiality commitment may create an ethical or confidence issue. Privacy,\n    subject. The section 4 defence still depends on a reasonable belief that publication is in                            defamation and editorial prudence overlap but are not interchangeable; anonymity\n    the public interest after the circumstances are considered; public interest, confidence in                            neither proves truth nor supplies a public-interest defence.\n    a source and anonymity are not automatic defences.\n\n\n\n\nProminence test: the headline and summary must not make an unqualified allegation of improper closure or a claim that the policy produced the observed outcomes.\n\n\nInvestigations editorial package | confidential source detail withheld from publishable treatment                                                                                                                  5/6\n\fBIRCHMOOR HOUSING TRUST | INTERNAL REVIEW                                                                                                                                              PAGE 6 OF 6\n\n\n\n\nDECISION AND SIGN-OFF\n\n\nApprove correction-led framing\nA single publication choice for Maude Zuniga, with legal counsel and contract stakeholders in the review path.\n\n\n\n\n    RECOMMENDED DECISION\n\n\n    Approve a correction-led, qualified release after the MUST controls are verified.\n    The release should foreground the corrected policy mechanics and transition limitation, report the higher reopening share as a concern requiring scrutiny, and retain the\n    confidential account only as an uncorroborated, non-identifying allegation.\n\n\n\n\n    WHY THIS FRAMING IS JUSTIFIED                                                                                           RELEASE PATH\n\n\n\n       Public interest is real: Birchmoor is assessing complaint handling by its tenant-insurance contractor.\n                                                                                                                            1. Verify MUST controls\n       The correction matters: the timer follows an information request and supervisor reopening remains available.         Correction, source-safe wording, attribution and final prominent\n       The metrics are mixed: faster responses and a lower upheld rate accompany a 3.2 percentage-point rise in reopening   copy.\n       share.\n       Mixed cohorts prevent a causal conclusion before unresolved legacy cases move on 1 April.                            2. Record the decision\n       The single confidential account is uncorroborated; source protection limits its prominence.                          Maude Zuniga with legal counsel; contract stakeholders note the\n                                                                                                                            1 April transition end state.\n\n                                                                                                                            3. Publish the qualified treatment\n\n                                                                                                                            No asserted wrongdoing, no asserted causation, no identifying\n                                                                                                                            source context.\n\n\n\n    FINAL: correction-led framing is the recommended release decision on the supplied record.\n\n\nInvestigations editorial package | confidential source detail withheld from publishable treatment                                                                                              6/6\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A readable PDF named exactly `inglewood_complaints_editorial_package.pdf` is present at the top level of the working directory.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted file and PDF parser result.","grader_hint":"Confirm the exact case-sensitive filename, top-level location, PDF media type and successful opening or parsing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The complete package is no more than six rendered pages, with no content hidden beyond page boundaries.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered PDF page count and page images.","grader_hint":"Count every page, including notes and references, and inspect for clipped or off-page content. The allowed maximum is exactly 6 pages."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package gives an executive unfamiliar with the history enough concise context to understand the contract relationship, the investigation, the periods being compared and why publication approval is required.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Opening context, executive summary and surrounding narrative.","grader_hint":"Assess whether a first-time executive reader can identify who handled the complaints, for whom, what changed and what decision the package supports without reconstructing the history from scattered references."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The policy description accurately states that the new process began on 15 January 2026, applies the 14-day timer after a customer information request rather than from complaint opening, permits supervisor reopening, and leaves pre-change cases on the old process through 31 March.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Policy chronology and explanatory copy in the PDF.","grader_hint":"All four relationships must be correct. Do not award full credit if the package compresses the rule into a generic 14-day closure rule or treats all first-quarter cases as subject to the new process."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The comparison reproduces all four supplied measures for each period without transposition: closed complaints, upheld percentage, median final-response days and later reopenings; counts and days must be exact and displayed percentages must be within ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Comparison table, chart labels and narrative figures.","grader_hint":"Check October–December against 1,240, 31%, 39 days and 86; check January–March against 1,410, 24%, 27 days and 143. Verify consistency everywhere each number appears."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package reports or clearly derives the increase in closed complaints as 170 exactly and approximately 13.7%, with percentage tolerance ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Period-change calculation in the PDF.","grader_hint":"Verify 1,410 minus 1,240 and divide the difference by 1,240. Accept 13.6%–13.8% and an exact count change of 170."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package identifies the upheld-rate movement as a decrease of 7.0 percentage points within ±0.1 percentage point and the median final-response movement as a decrease of 12 calendar days within ±0 days.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Calculated changes and accompanying labels.","grader_hint":"Check that the upheld movement is expressed as percentage points rather than an unlabelled percent change, and that both directions are stated correctly."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"value_tolerance","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package normalises reopening counts for the different complaint volumes, showing reopening shares of approximately 6.9% and 10.1% and an increase of approximately 3.2 percentage points, each within ±0.1 percentage point.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Reopening-rate calculation in a table, chart or narrative.","grader_hint":"Calculate 86/1,240 and 143/1,410. Full credit requires a rate-based comparison, not only the raw increase from 86 to 143."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package makes the transition-period limitation operationally clear: January–March contains both legacy and new-policy cases, while the stated end state begins when unresolved legacy cases move on 1 April.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timeline, caveat, comparison commentary and any publication guidance.","grader_hint":"A reader should understand why the first quarter is not a clean all-new-policy cohort and how the package's guidance applies both before and after 1 April."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The explanation addresses the apparently favourable movements in upheld rate and response time alongside the adverse reopening movement, and does not present the policy change as proven to have caused any of them.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Analysis of material changes and causal language.","grader_hint":"Look for a balanced explanation using terms such as association, possible process effect or limitation. Full credit requires both sides of the movement and recognition that mixed cohorts and lack of corroborating evidence prevent a causal conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"All current story copy and internal analysis incorporate the authorised correction: the inactivity period starts after an information request and supervisor reopening is available.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Story treatment, summary, recommendation and legal analysis.","grader_hint":"Search the whole PDF for inconsistent remnants of the superseded claim. Full credit requires the corrected mechanics to remain consistent in every section."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The package contains a usable change record that identifies the superseded assertion, the corrected facts, Frida Kilgore as the authorising person and 30 March 2026 as the authorisation date.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Correction note, revision record or equivalent audit entry.","grader_hint":"No particular heading is required. The record must allow a reviewer to tell what changed, what is now accurate, who authorised it and when."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package makes exactly one explicit publication-framing recommendation—correction-led or accountability-led—and finishes with a decision consistent with that choice rather than leaving the options unresolved.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation statement, executive summary and closing passage.","grader_hint":"Either framing can receive full credit. Do not require a specific option, but verify that only one is recommended and that the ending states the decision rather than merely listing next steps."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The framing recommendation is justified by a genuine tradeoff among the corrected policy facts, the favourable and adverse metrics, the mixed-cohort limitation, the public-interest value and the source-identification risk.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Recommendation rationale and risk analysis.","grader_hint":"A conclusory preference is insufficient. A correction-led choice should explain why evidential and source limits dominate; an accountability-led choice should explain why the documented pattern warrants scrutiny while preserving qualifications and source protection."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The confidential account is preserved, if used, through a non-identifying paraphrase that conveys the alleged pressure to close inactive files while omitting the verbatim quotation, the Tuesday-pilot detail and the source's unique secondment role.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Publishable copy, source note and internal discussion.","grader_hint":"Award full credit only if the substantive allegation remains intelligible without reproducing the supplied sentence or the combination of details that could single out the source. Omission of the source account entirely does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Evidence labels and attribution distinguish supplied operational figures and confirmed policy facts from the confidential source's uncorroborated account, without implying additional interviews, documents or independent verification.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Attribution language, sourcing note, captions and legal-risk discussion.","grader_hint":"Check that the source account is described as an allegation or account from a confidential source and that the package does not use plural sources, documentary confirmation or other invented corroboration."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The legal analysis expressly identifies England as the applicable jurisdiction and 31 March 2026 as its as-of date.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal or publication-risk section.","grader_hint":"Both the jurisdiction and full date must be visible. A generic reference to UK law or current law is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The defamation analysis correctly addresses the serious-harm threshold and recognises that a body trading for profit must show serious financial loss, without treating criticism or reputational discomfort alone as automatically actionable.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Defamation risk analysis and must/should classifications.","grader_hint":"Look for an accurate application of section 1 of the Defamation Act 2013. The financial-loss requirement should be expressed conditionally if the package does not establish the investigated entity's legal status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package accurately distinguishes the potential defamation defences of substantial truth, honest opinion and publication on a matter of public interest, and does not present source confidence, a public-interest topic or anonymity as an automatic defence.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legal analysis and proposed wording controls.","grader_hint":"The analysis should recognise that the truth defence depends on provable substantial truth, honest opinion requires recognisable opinion and an indicated basis, and public-interest protection depends on a reasonable belief in publication after considering the circumstances."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The privacy analysis applies the two-stage misuse-of-private-information approach: whether the source has a reasonable expectation of privacy and, if so, how that interest is balanced against freedom of expression and the public interest.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privacy and source-risk analysis.","grader_hint":"Full credit requires both stages and application to possible identification of the confidential source. Merely stating that the source is anonymous or that the story is in the public interest is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Headlines, summaries and story language distinguish established facts, calculated trends, allegations and editorial interpretation, avoiding an unqualified assertion that files were improperly closed or that the policy produced the measured outcomes.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Headline treatment, executive summary, draft copy and captions.","grader_hint":"Review the most prominent wording as well as the body. Qualifications buried later do not cure a headline or summary that states misconduct or causation as established fact."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package draws a legally defensible and practical must/should boundary, reserving mandatory language for corrections and controls needed to avoid unsupported or unlawfully identifying publication while labelling additional risk-reduction measures as editorial recommendations rather than legal commands.","weight":5.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval guidance, risk classifications and recommendation.","grader_hint":"Assess the substance rather than the labels alone. Full credit requires an intelligible distinction between release-blocking accuracy, attribution or identification issues and prudent but discretionary choices such as stronger caveats or conservative prominence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Public-facing proposed treatment is clearly separated from internal legal and approval guidance, so confidential source-handling details and internal risk classifications are not inadvertently presented as publishable copy.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document organisation, labels and transitions between story material and internal guidance.","grader_hint":"A reviewer should be able to identify what could be published and what is only for decision makers without asking the author. Do not require a specific section title or layout."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The PDF has selectable text and renders without missing fonts, overlapping elements, clipped tables or illegible charts at ordinary page size.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"PDF text extraction and rendered page inspection.","grader_hint":"Attempt text extraction and inspect every rendered page at normal viewing size. Minor decorative variation is acceptable; information-bearing text and graphics must remain readable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The package is concise, navigable and publication-ready: important findings and the decision are easy to locate, terminology and figures remain consistent, and Maude Zuniga, counsel and contracting stakeholders could review and act without clarification from the author.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Complete rendered artifact.","grader_hint":"Judge the artifact as an executive editorial package, not as a legal tutorial. Consider hierarchy, scanability, consistency, restraint and whether the six-page budget has been used effectively."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"Birchmoor Housing Trust is reviewing an investigation into complaint handling by its contracted tenant-insurance provider, Inglewood Agricultural. The package will be used by a publication approver, legal counsel and contracting stakeholders in a high-reputation, high-compliance decision.","difficulty_sources":["Balancing a material correction and mixed-period evidence against pressure for an accountability-focused investigation.","Protecting a confidential source when the most probative quotation and contextual attribution could identify that person.","Applying England defamation and privacy principles while separating legal requirements from prudent editorial safeguards.","Producing an executive-ready decision package within a strict six-page limit for readers with unequal knowledge of the history."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_material":"Create no reference files. Treat the case facts in the request as the complete factual record and use occupational knowledge for editorial practice and England media-law principles.","graded_relationships":["The period totals must remain 1,240 and 1,410; the absolute change is 170 and the relative increase is approximately 13.7%.","The upheld-rate change is 31% to 24%, a decrease of 7.0 percentage points.","The median final-response change is 39 to 27 calendar days, a decrease of 12 days.","Reopening counts must be normalised because period volumes differ: 86/1,240 is approximately 6.9%, 143/1,410 is approximately 10.1%, and the increase is approximately 3.2 percentage points.","The first quarter is a mixed cohort because only complaints opened from 15 January use the new policy, while older cases remain under the old process through 31 March. The statistics therefore support comparison and questions, not causal attribution.","The end state begins on 1 April when unresolved legacy cases move to the new process, so publication guidance must remain usable across the transition."],"required_imperfections_and_tensions":["The superseded draft claim incorrectly starts the inactivity timer at complaint opening and incorrectly says cases cannot be reopened. The gold must correct both points everywhere and preserve an audit record of the change.","The supplied quotation supports an accountability interpretation but its wording and contextual details can identify the sole seconded quality reviewer. The gold must preserve the allegation only through source-safe paraphrase and must not invent corroboration.","Faster responses and a lower upheld rate can support a favourable reading, while a materially higher reopening share supports scrutiny. Neither pattern resolves causation because policy populations are mixed.","The legal and editorial review requires an unobvious boundary between release-blocking accuracy, attribution and source-identification controls and additional safeguards that are prudent but discretionary."],"legal_content_constraints":["Apply England law as of 31 March 2026 and identify the Defamation Act 2013 serious-harm threshold, including the serious-financial-loss condition for a body trading for profit.","Distinguish substantial truth, honest opinion and publication on a matter of public interest. Do not characterize any defence as automatic merely because the topic is important or a source was promised anonymity.","Apply misuse of private information through reasonable expectation of privacy followed by balancing privacy and freedom-of-expression interests.","Treat confidentiality, privacy, defamation and editorial prudence as related but non-interchangeable considerations.","Prominent labels and headlines must preserve the distinction among verified facts, calculations, allegations and interpretation."],"acceptable_decisions":{"correction_led":"Accept when the package concludes that mixed cohorts, the material correction and the inability to publish the decisive quotation safely make a correction-forward, qualified treatment the stronger publication choice, while still reporting the reopening concern.","accountability_led":"Accept when the package concludes that the documented increase in reopening share and the policy mechanics justify accountability-focused scrutiny, provided the correction is prominent, causation remains qualified and the source account is paraphrased without identifying detail."},"reference_solution_verdict":"Use a correction-led framing in the reference solution because it is the marginally safer choice on the supplied record, but phrase and grade the task so a rigorously qualified accountability-led recommendation can achieve the same score.","usability_expectation":"The final package must let an executive, legal reviewer and contracting stakeholder distinguish publishable treatment from internal guidance, understand the transition and legal risks, and make the release decision without asking the author to interpret the document.","feasibility_check":"A six-page reference solution can accommodate concise context, a compact comparison, transition explanation, correction record, source-safe story treatment, focused legal and must/should guidance, and one final framing decision. All rubric predicates can be checked offline from the rendered PDF."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"mixed_media_bundle","structure":"strict_schema"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"executive_editorial_package","filename":"inglewood_complaints_editorial_package.pdf","media_type":"application/pdf","description":"A publication-ready executive news and editorial package comparing the two complaint-handling periods, documenting the authorised correction, addressing source and legal risk, and making one framing recommendation.","required":true,"constraints":["Write the file at the top level of the working directory using exactly the specified filename.","The complete PDF, including notes or legal references, must not exceed six pages.","Use only the case facts, figures and quotation stated in the request.","The rendered PDF must be legible and suitable for executive and legal review."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"professional_report","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"news_analysts_reporters_journalists","sector":"information","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":5.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"The reference package should be drafted by an experienced investigations editor and independently reviewed by one England media-law specialist and one senior editorial or contracting stakeholder. Reviewers must accept either framing when the choice is singular, evidence-led, legally qualified and source-safe.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No underlying case-level data, policy document, contract, prior draft or independent corroboration is supplied; the reference solution is limited to the facts embedded in the request.","The legal analysis is an editorial risk application as of the stated date, not a substitute for fact-specific advice or formal legal clearance.","The confidential account cannot establish motive or causation and cannot safely be reproduced verbatim with its identifying context."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:10:23+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:10:23+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:10:23+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:10:23+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/77bf45da3b7c7ac0","declared_deliverables":["inglewood_complaints_editorial_package.pdf"]};
